      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the probability that two randomly chosen members of a club (with 5 boys and 5 girls) are both girls, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10.**\n\n   The number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10 is given by the combination formula:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n   \\]\n   where \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the factorials:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = \\frac{90}{2} = 45\n   \\]\n\n   So, there are 45 ways to choose 2 members out of 10.\n\n2. **Determine the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5.**\n\n   Similarly, the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of 5 is:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!}\n   \\]\n\n   Simplify the factorials:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = \\frac{20}{2} = 10\n   \\]\n\n   So, there are 10 ways to choose 2 girls out of 5.\n\n3. **Calculate the probability that both chosen members are girls.**\n\n   The probability is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes (choosing 2 girls) to the total number of outcomes (choosing any 2 members):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of ways to choose 2 girls}}{\\text{Total number of ways to choose 2 members}}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the values we found:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Probability} = \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{2}{9}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that both chosen members are girls is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\\).",
