      "text": "To find the probability that both chosen members are girls, we can use the concept of combinations. We'll start by determining the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10. This can be calculated using the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of items, \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of the 5 girls. This is given by:\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n\\]\n\nThe probability that both chosen members are girls is the number of ways to choose 2 girls divided by the total number of ways to choose 2 members from 10:\n\\[\nP(\\text{both girls}) = \\frac{\\binom{5}{2}}{\\binom{10}{2}} = \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{2}{9}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that both chosen members are girls is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\\).",
