      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the probability that two randomly chosen members from a club of 10 members (5 boys and 5 girls) are both girls.\n\nFirst, we calculate the total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10. This can be done using the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!} \\), where \\( n \\) is the total number of items, \\( k \\) is the number of items to choose, and \\( ! \\) denotes factorial.\n\nThe total number of ways to choose 2 members out of 10 is:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{2} = \\frac{10!}{2!(10-2)!} = \\frac{10!}{2! \\cdot 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 8!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9}{2 \\times 1} = 45\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the number of ways to choose 2 girls out of the 5 girls. This is also given by the combination formula, where \\( n = 5 \\) and \\( k = 2 \\):\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{2} = \\frac{5!}{2!(5-2)!} = \\frac{5!}{2! \\cdot 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 3!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4}{2 \\times 1} = 10\n\\]\n\nThe probability that both chosen members are girls is the number of ways to choose 2 girls divided by the total number of ways to choose 2 members from 10:\n\\[\n\\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\binom{5}{2}}{\\binom{10}{2}} = \\frac{10}{45} = \\frac{2}{9}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that the two chosen members are both girls is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{2}{9}}\n\\]\n\nEvaluate $\\lceil0.9\\rceil+\\lfloor2.1\\rfloor$.\nTo solve the problem \\(\\lceil0.9\\rceil + \\lfloor2.1\\rfloor\\), we need to evaluate the ceiling and floor functions separately and then sum the results.\n\n1. **Evaluate \\(\\lceil0.9\\rceil\\):**\n   The ceiling function \\(\\lceil x \\rceil\\) returns the smallest integer greater than or equal to \\(x\\). For \\(x = 0.9\\):\n   \\[\n   \\lceil0.9\\rceil = 1\n   \\]\n\n2. **Evaluate \\(\\lfloor2.1\\rfloor\\):**\n   The floor function \\(\\lfloor x \\rfloor\\) returns the largest integer less than or equal to \\(x\\). For \\(x = 2.1\\):\n   \\[\n   \\lfloor2.1\\rfloor = 2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sum the results:**\n   Now, we add the results of the ceiling and floor functions:\n   \\[\n   \\lceil0.9\\rceil + \\lfloor2.1\\rfloor = 1 + 2 = 3\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{3}\n\\]\n\nHow many diagonals can be drawn for a hexagon?\nA hexagon has 6 vertices. The formula to calculate the number of diagonals in an \\(n\\)-sided polygon is given by:\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-3)}{2}\n\\]\nFor a hexagon, \\(n = 6\\). Plugging in the value of \\(n\\), we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{6(6-3)}{2} = \\frac{6 \\times 3}{2} = \\frac{18}{2} = 9\n\\]\nThus, the number of diagonals that can be drawn in a hexagon is \\(\\boxed{9}\\).\n\nWhat is the units digit of $23^{23}$?\n\nTo find the units digit of \\(23^{23}\\), we need to determine the units digit of the number \\(23\\) when raised to the power of \\(23\\). This is equivalent to finding the units digit of \\(3^{23}\\) because the units digit of a product is determined only by the units digits of the factors.\n\nThe units digits of the powers of \\(3\\) cycle in a pattern:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n3^1 &\\equiv 3 \\pmod{10}, \\\\\n3^2 &\\equiv 9 \\pmod{10}, \\\\\n3^3 &\\equiv 27 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{10}, \\\\\n3^4 &\\equiv 81 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{10}, \\\\\n3^5 &\\equiv 3 \\pmod{10}, \\quad \\text{(and the cycle repeats every 4 powers)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSo, the units digit of \\(3^n\\) depends on the remainder when \\(n\\) is divided by \\(4\\). Specifically, the units digit of \\(3^n\\) is \\(3\\) if \\(n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}\\), \\(9\\) if \\(n \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}\\), \\(7\\) if \\(n \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\\), and \\(1\\) if \\(n \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}\\).\n\nSince \\(23 \\div 4 = 5\\) with a remainder of \\(3\\), we have \\(23 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\\). Therefore, the units digit of \\(3^{23}\\) is the same as the units digit of \\(3^3\\), which is \\(7\\).\n\nThus, the units digit of \\(23^{23}\\) is \\(\\boxed{7}\\).",
